  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C4 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">When a sensitive child falls apart in a crowded, noisy place, their brain may be sounding the same alarm it would sound for real danger.<br><br>When sensory input piles up, the brain's threat system can fire and launch the body into fight, flight, or freeze. That is why a child might lash out, try to run, or shut down completely. The brain, flooded with input, cannot always tell the difference between overwhelming and dangerous. It is not a decision. It is an automatic survival response taking over.<br><br>Which is exactly why meeting that moment with a consequence rarely helps. You cannot discipline a brain out of an alarm state. You can only help it feel safe enough to come back down. Overload is protection, not defiance.</div>
    <div class="caption-tags">#sensoryoverload #fightflightfreeze #threatresponse</div>
    <div class="caption-accounts">Tag: none needed</div>
