  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C5 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">Here is something the brain normally does so well you never notice it: it gets used to things.<br><br>Most brains quiet their response to a steady, repeating input, a humming light, a clothing seam, over the first minute or so. Researchers call this habituation. But in some children, that habituation is reduced. The input never fades into the background the way it is supposed to. It stays just as loud and just as present on minute ten as it was on minute one.<br><br>So when your child is still bothered by the seam or the buzz long after you have stopped noticing it, they are not being stubborn or dramatic. Their brain genuinely is not tuning it out. It never fades for them the way it does for you.</div>
